        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.271,
            0.081,
            0.533,
            0.134
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Does the difference in air pressure have anything to do with the formation of winds?"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.558,
            0.094,
            0.749,
            0.113
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "How do winds form?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.285,
            0.154,
            0.698,
            0.175
          ],
          "content": "Let us find out from the following activity."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.804,
            0.064,
            0.9,
            0.169
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_8_image_5.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.27,
            0.197,
            0.627,
            0.22
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Activity 6.5: Let us observe"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.061,
            0.232,
            0.367,
            0.332
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.095,
                0.339,
                0.332,
                0.388
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 6.12: Air moves from a high pressure region to a low pressure region"
            }
          ],
          "image_source": "images/page_8_image_7.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.381,
            0.236,
            0.882,
            0.275
          ],
          "content": "z Take two similar balloons made of thin rubber, and a drinking straw."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.382,
            0.277,
            0.883,
            0.315
          ],
          "content": "z Insert one end of the straw into one balloon and secure it with a rubber band or thread."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.382,
            0.318,
            0.882,
            0.356
          ],
          "content": "z Now inflate the second balloon and hold its mouth with your fingers, so that air does not escape."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.383,
            0.358,
            0.884,
            0.395
          ],
          "content": "z Insert the free end of the straw into the neck of the inflated balloon and secure it with a rubber"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.294,
            0.395,
            0.883,
            0.47
          ],
          "content": "band or thread. Make sure that the air does not leak from the balloon as the straw is inserted in it. Now you have one end of the straw inside the inflated balloon and the other end inside the uninflated balloon as shown in Fig. 6.12."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.273,
            0.472,
            0.729,
            0.492
          ],
          "content": "**Predict** what would happen to the balloons."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.273,
            0.495,
            0.882,
            0.533
          ],
          "content": "Observe what happens to both the balloons. Did it happen as predicted?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.273,
            0.535,
            0.882,
            0.574
          ],
          "content": "z Do you observe any change in the size of the balloons? Write down your observations."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.254,
            0.605,
            0.883,
            0.699
          ],
          "content": "What can be the reason for the change in the sizes of the balloons? The air pressure in the inflated balloon is higher than that in the uninflated balloon. As a result, some air moves from the inflated balloon to the uninflated balloon, resulting in changes in the size of both the balloons."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.254,
            0.7,
            0.884,
            0.849
          ],
          "content": "Do you notice that after some time both the balloons attain almost the same size and the flow of air stops? Why does the air flow stop? The flow of air continues till the air pressure in the inflated balloon is higher than the air pressure in the uninflated balloon. The air flow stops when the pressure in both balloons becomes equal. At this stage, both balloons are almost of the same size. Thus, we can conclude that air moves from a region of high air pressure to a region of low air pressure."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.254,
            0.849,
            0.883,
            0.907
          ],
          "content": "You can relate this conclusion to the directions of the sea breeze and land breeze, which you studied in Curiosity, Grade 7. As land gets heated faster than water during the day, the air above the land"
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "bbox": [
            0.001,
            0.927,
            0.16,
            1.0
          ],
          "content": "88 PR"
        },
